Outside the Permitted Options
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 
The Department of Remaining Possibilities occupied the second and part of the third floor of a building in Marsham Street that had been designed in 1971 for a purpose nobody could now establish, and it had, after nineteen months, forty-one staff, a budget
, a mission statement Neville Wren had refused to write, and a corridor smell.
\par The corridor smell was the thing that had finally convinced him it was real.
\par You could found an institution, he had discovered, and appoint to it, and give it powers, and it would remain somehow provisional \emdash  a thing being tried \emdash 
 right up until the morning you walked in and recognised the smell of the corridor as belonging to you
. After that it was permanent, and had always been permanent, and would outlive everyone in it, and would in the fullness of time do something appalling that nobody currently employed would have countenanced.
\par He had put that observation in the notebook, which by then was in its fourth volume, and Stephanie Fainrose had read it and said, "Yes. Now write the mission statement."
\par He had not written the mission statement.
\par The department's work was, in the language of the Act, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 the identification, monitoring, and protection of unresolved states within the civil estate.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  
In practice it meant three things. It meant reviewing every proposed instrument that touched a citizen's affairs and asking whether it demanded conjugate certainties, which was now unlawful and which departments still attempted roughly eleven times a mont
h, mostly through sincere ignorance. It meant maintaining the register of the three hundred and eight \emdash 
 the P-9 states recovered from the horizon of Singularity Asset 4C during the eleven weeks, returned to the districts they had come out of, and not one of them yet finished.
\par And it meant Thursdays.
\par Thursdays were Anomalies.
\par "Wren."
\par Miss Adaeze Pike put her head round the door at nine forty on a Thursday morning in the second week of March, and Neville knew from her face that Thursday was not going to be routine, because Miss Pike had spent nineteen months becoming the best analyst i
n the building and had entirely lost the brittle brightness she had arrived with and had acquired instead a very level way of delivering bad news.
\par "P-9 register," she said. "One of them's resolved."
\par "That's good."
\par "No it isn't."
\par She came in and put a single sheet on his desk, which was the departmental convention for }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 this cannot be sent by email yet.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 
\par "Number one-forty-one," she said. "Ellen Prosper, aged thirty-four, Sub-District 6, Coldharrow Rise. Pending state at time of transfer: whether to accept a place on a postgraduate course in Newcastle or remain and take over her mother's shop."
\par Neville knew it. He knew all three hundred and eight, which was not a policy, merely a thing that had happened to him.
\par "She's decided?"
\par "She's resolved," said Miss Pike, "into neither."
\par Neville looked up.
\par "Say that properly."
\par "She is not in Newcastle. She has not taken the shop. As of Tuesday at approximately four in the afternoon, Ellen Prosper is a licensed operator of a small marine survey vessel out of Peterhead, in a partnership with two people she had never met, in a tra
de she had \emdash  and I have checked this six ways \emdash  no prior knowledge of, no contact with, no qualification for, and no route to." Miss Pike put a second sheet down. "The state resolved outside its permitted options."
\par Neville sat back very slowly.
\par "That's not possible," he said. "That's \emdash  Adaeze, that's not a slightly surprising outcome. A pending state has a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 set}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 . It's a superposition over the alternatives that were live when it was frozen. Two options in, one option out. That's what resolution }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 is}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 ."
\par "Yes."
\par "There is no amplitude on Peterhead."
\par "No," said Miss Pike. "There is no amplitude on Peterhead."
\par "Then either the register is wrong about what her options were\emdash "
\par "It isn't. It's her own declaration, countersigned, and she is quite clear that in November of the year before last she had two possibilities and hated both."
\par "\emdash or she simply changed her mind, in the ordinary way, like a person, over nineteen months, which is allowed and is in fact the entire point of what we gave back to them."
\par Miss Pike had been waiting for that, and Neville saw that she had been waiting for it, and understood that the morning was about to get worse.
\par "That's what I assumed on Tuesday," she said. "So I ran the coherence trace. Standard post-recovery monitoring, we do it monthly on all three hundred and eight, it's the dullest job in the building." She put down a third sheet, and this one was a graph. "
If somebody changes their mind, the state resolves \emdash 
 the amplitude moves from one option to the other and then it settles, and the trace goes flat and stays flat. That's what a decision looks like. We've got two hundred and eleven of them on file. They all look the same."
\par "And this one?"
\par "This one didn't move."
\par Neville looked at the graph.
\par The two options \emdash  Newcastle, and the shop \emdash  sat where they had sat for nineteen months, in the shallow untroubled way that unresolved things sit. Neither had grown. Neither had won.
\par And then, on the fourteenth, both of them had simply gone out, together, at the same instant, and something entirely else had come up somewhere off the edge of the chart.
\par "It didn't choose," said Neville.
\par "It didn't choose. It }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 left}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 
." Miss Pike's voice stayed level. "Both options extinguished simultaneously and a third appeared that was not in the state space. That's not a decision, sir. There's no mechanism in anything Dr Fainrose has ever written that permits it."
\par Neville was already reaching for the telephone.
\par "Are there others?"
\par "That," said Miss Pike, "is the reason I brought paper."
\par Fainrose came over from the oversight body at eleven and stood looking at the wall for a long time.
\par They had put them all up: fourteen coherence traces, pinned in a row along the plasterboard of the third-floor briefing room, and every one of them did the same thing. Two options, flat, patient, nineteen months of nothing. And then both extinguished with
in the same six-hour window, on the fourteenth of March, and something appearing outside the set.
\par "Fourteen out of three hundred and eight," said Fainrose.
\par "So far," said Miss Pike. "I've got the rest running. It takes two hours a trace."
\par "Are they geographically clustered?"
\par "No. Eleven districts, three counties."
\par "Demographically?"
\par "No."
\par "Related by content? Similar decisions?"
\par "No. A course, a shop, two marriages, an apprenticeship, a house purchase, a name change, a reconciliation, a decision about a dog." Miss Pike consulted nothing; she also knew all three hundred and eight. "There is exactly one thing they have in common an
d I don't like it."
\par "Go on."
\par "They were the fourteen that came out of the horizon }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 last}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 
," said Miss Pike. "In the final nine days of the recovery window, when 4C was in its terminal phase and von Wittenberg's people were unscrambling faster and dirtier than they wanted to. The last fourteen recovered. Nothing else connects them."
\par Fainrose said nothing at all for about half a minute.
\par Then she said, "Neville. What does a state do when it cannot resolve into any of its permitted options?"
\par "Nothing. It waits. It stays in superposition until something couples to it."
\par "And if it waits nineteen months, and the barrier between it and its permitted options is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 high}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  
\emdash  if the two available outcomes have both become, in some sense, unreachable \emdash  what then?"
\par "Then it goes on waiting."
\par "Classically, yes." Fainrose turned round. "Neville, you spent forty years in electronics. What is a tunnel diode."
\par And Neville felt the floor of nineteen months slide, in the way it had slid in a car park and on a wall and in a school hall, and he said, "Oh."
\par "Say it."
\par "A barrier the thing shouldn't get through," he said slowly, "and it gets through anyway. Not over. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 Through}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 . Because the amplitude doesn't stop at the wall \emdash 
 it drops off inside it, but it doesn't reach zero, and if the wall is thin enough there's a bit of it on the other side, and so there's a probability of finding the thing somewhere it did not have the energy to reach."
\par "Yes."
\par "You can't tunnel to a state that isn't in the space."
\par "You cannot tunnel to a state that is not in }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 your description}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  
of the space," said Fainrose. "Which is a different sentence, and the difference has cost this country nine thousand houses once already."
\par Miss Pike said, "Meaning what, exactly?"
\par "Meaning that our register lists two options for Ellen Prosper because Ellen Prosper listed two options for Ellen Prosper," said Fainrose, "and both of us may have been describing a subspace of something larger. And if the true state space is larger, then
 there was always a barrier, and there was always a small amplitude on the far side of it, and it was always negligible." She looked at the wall of traces. "Something has made it not negligible. In fourteen cases. In a six-hour window. And barriers do not
 thin themselves."
\par The briefing room was quiet.
\par "There's one more thing," said Miss Pike, "and I've been saving it because I wanted you both in the room."
\par She took out a fourth sheet, folded, from her inside pocket.
\par "Ellen Prosper's survey vessel," she said. "It's a real business, it's registered, it's insured, it's boring. The two partners are a man from Aberdeen and a woman from Sligo. Both of them have full and unremarkable histories and neither of them has ever b
een near a P-9 register." She unfolded the sheet. "But the contract that put the three of them together \emdash  the initial charter, the thing that got them into a room \emdash 
 was let by a client through an agent in Vienna, and the client is a holding company, and the holding company has one named director."
\par Neville took the sheet.
\par He read the name.
\par Then he put it down on the briefing room table with considerable care, in the manner of a man setting down something that might still be live.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 H. Weinstein.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 
\par "He said there would be other centuries," said Neville.
\par "He said," said Fainrose, "that they had stopped asking on an industrial scale, and that it was a famine, and that he did not mind as much as he expected." She was looking at the fourteen traces, and her shoulders had not come down, and Neville had learne
d nineteen months ago what that meant. "He has been on short rations since the fourteenth of November the year before last, and he is eleven thousand years old, and he has just been found chartering a survey vessel."
\par Miss Pike said, "Surveying what?"
\par Nobody answered her, because at that moment the telephone in the briefing room rang, which it never did, and Neville picked it up, and listened, and said "yes" twice and "when" once and "no, keep it off the system" and put it down.
\par "That was the observatory liaison," he said.
\par "We have an observatory liaison?"
\par "We have a form that generates one." He was already gathering the traces off the wall. "Adaeze, get the remaining traces running two at a time and I don't care what it does to the machine. Stephanie \emdash 
 the fourteenth of March. Six-hour window. Between about two in the morning and eight."
\par "Yes."
\par "They've had a detection." He got his coat. "Nothing dramatic, nothing that made the news, they've been arguing about calibration for three weeks because nobody wanted to file it. A coincident event across four instruments. Not a signal, they're very clea
r it isn't a signal, they were quite insistent about that."
\par Fainrose said, "Then what is it."
\par "They don't have a word," said Neville. "The best the man could manage was that for a period of about six hours, in a particular direction, the vacuum appeared to be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 cheaper}
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  than it ought to be."
\par He held the door.
\par "Direction?"
\par "Out," said Neville Wren. "Along the arm. Nothing local at all."
