\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 quantum tunnelling}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 , which is the gentlest possible entry into where the sequel is going.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 The effect.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  
Classically, a particle with less energy than a potential barrier cannot cross it: it arrives, it is reflected, that is the end of the matter. Quantum mechanically the wavefunction does not terminate at the barrier's edge. Inside a classically forbidden r
egion the solution to the Schr\'f6
dinger equation becomes exponentially decaying rather than oscillatory, and if the barrier is thin enough, the amplitude on the far side is small but nonzero. The particle therefore has a finite probability of being found beyo
nd an obstacle it never had the energy to climb. It does not go over. It does not tunnel a hole. The amplitude was simply never zero anywhere.
\par The transmission probability falls off roughly as
