which is why the effect is exquisitely sensitive to barrier width and height: thin barriers are practically transparent, and a barrier twice as thick can suppress transmission by many orders of magnitude. It is not exotic. It is why the Sun shines (proton
s fuse only by tunnelling through their mutual electrostatic repulsion), why alpha decay happens at all, why the tunnel diode and the scanning tunnelling microscope work, and why flash memory can be written and erased.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 Why it is the right opening idea.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  
Book One's physics was about states being forced, measured, frozen, and correlated. Tunnelling introduces the opposite anxiety: a system arriving somewhere it had no permitted route to. And it does so without any appeal to interpretation \emdash 
 tunnelling is uncontroversial, textbook, and technologically exploited, so nobody in the story can dismiss it as metaphysics.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 The distinction the chapter turns on.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  
A state cannot resolve into an outcome that is not in its state space. But "the state space" in any practical account is a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 model}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 , and a truncated model has an edge where the real space does not. Fainrose's correction \emdash  you cannot tunnel to a state that is not in your }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 description}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  of the space \emdash 
 is the sequel's governing suspicion, and it is a direct escalation of Book One's central error. The Ministry once mistook a subspace for reality and flattened houses into it. The new question is whether everyone, including the people trying
 to fix it, has been doing a milder version of the same thing all along.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 Where it points.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 
 Tunnelling has a cosmological cousin. If the vacuum state we occupy is not the lowest available one but merely a local minimum \emdash  a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645 false vacuum}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid1972645  \emdash 
 then it is metastable, and it can decay by tunnelling to a lower state, nucleating a bubble that expands at nearly the speed of light. Whether our vacuum is stable, metastable, or unstable depends on parameters including the Higgs and top quark masses, 
and current measurements sit uncomfortably close to the boundary between stability and metastability. The relevant timescales, on standard estimates, are enormously longer than the present age of the universe.
\par The observatory's non-signal \emdash  six hours in which the vacuum appeared, in one direction, to be cheaper than it ought to be \emdash 
 is the sequel's first quiet suggestion that a barrier somewhere got thinner, and that fourteen people in eleven districts went somewhere they had no route to.
