The observatory liaison generated by the form turned out to be a Dr Priya Ramanathan, who met them at the gate of a facility outside Cheltenham that had been built to do radio astronomy and now did four other things as well, none of which it advertised.

\par She looked at Neville's identification for slightly too long.
\par "Remaining Possibilities," she said.
\par "Yes."
\par "Is that a real department."
\par "There's a corridor smell," said Neville. "You get used to the question."
\par Inside, she took them to a room in which four men and a woman were conducting, with great courtesy, the sort of argument that has been going on so long that everybody has memorised everybody else's position and is now arguing chiefly out of loyalty to the
ir earlier selves.
\par "Three weeks," said Dr Ramanathan. "We've been at it three weeks. Nobody will sign the note."
\par Fainrose said, "Show us the event."
\par They showed them the event.
\par It was, as advertised, not a signal. There was no structure in it, no pulse, no repetition, nothing that could be read. What there was, across four instruments of three different kinds \emdash 
