\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 Chapter Three carries }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 determinism versus irreducible chance}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
, and the specific methodological claim behind Einstein's most-quoted and most-misused sentence.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 What Einstein actually objected to.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  
In a 1926 letter to Max Born he wrote that quantum theory "delivers much, but it hardly brings us closer to the Old One's secret. I am at any rate convinced that }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 He}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
 does not play dice." The remark is routinely misread as a mystic's discomfort with randomness. His actual objection was methodological and rather dry: quantum mechanics yields probabilities, and Einstein held that a probability is what a theory offers wh
en its description is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 incomplete}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
. Statistical mechanics gives probabilities for a gas, but nobody thinks a molecule's trajectory is undetermined \emdash 
 the statistics are a confession of ignorance about the microstate. Einstein's position was that the Born rule occupies the same epistemic ro
le and that the underlying variables remain to be found. This is the position Weinstein articulates, and it is respectable as method even where it fails as physics.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 Hidden variables, and where Einstein was wrong.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  
The 1935 EPR paper argued that quantum mechanics must be incomplete on the grounds that one can predict with certainty the result of a distant measurement without disturbing the distant system \emdash 
 implying a pre-existing element of reality the formalism omits. The argument assumed }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 locality}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
. Bell's theorem (Book One, Chapter Thirteen) later showed that locality and pre-existing definite properties cannot both survive, and experiment has ruled against their conjunction. Einstein was therefore wrong about locality. He was not thereby shown wr
ong that a completion exists: pilot-wave theory (Book One, Chapter Twelve) is a fully deterministic hidden-variable account that reproduces all quantum predictions, at the price of explicit nonlocality. In such a theory, outcomes that look random are fixe
d by initial positions we do not know.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 The chapter's actual test, and why it is not hand-waving.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
 Weinstein does not assert determinism; he infers it from an observable. His claim is that a genuinely stochastic transition has a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 cost}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  \emdash  branches not taken, alternatives shed, entropy produced \emdash 
 whereas a determined transition, one in which nothing else could have occurred, sheds nothing. This is a real distinction with real physical shape:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls6\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid5709733 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 Landauer's principle}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  establishes that logically irreversible operations have a thermodynamic cost, dissipating at least }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 k_B T \\ln 2}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  per bit erased. Selection }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 among}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  live alternatives is exactly such an operation.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f3\fs20\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 \loch\af3\dbch\af0\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}
A transition with only one available final state is not a selection at all. There is nothing to discard and nothing to erase.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
So "no leavings" is not mysticism dressed as evidence; it is an entropy-accounting argument, and it descends directly from Kastner's rule in Book One \emdash  order is never free, and if nothing was paid, nothing was chosen.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 The tunnelling mechanism underneath.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
 Chapter One established that transmission depends exponentially on the barrier. What Fainrose recognises here is the other side of that expression: a well confines a state only if the states it confines it }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 to}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  are physically available. The register maintained fourteen superpositions over options that had ceased to exist \emdash 
 the file said two doors, and physically both were rock. In such a case the confinement is administrative rather than energetic, and the barri
er separating the state from everything outside the register's description is already vanishingly thin. A six-hour reduction in the vacuum floor did not push anyone. It simply removed the last resistance from the only fourteen states in the country that h
ad nothing holding them up. The distribution of outcomes was not sampled; it was }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 drained}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 .
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 The caveat worth keeping.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
 The chapter should not be read as settling the century-old question. It is entirely possible that quantum indeterminacy is fundamental and irreducible, and the mainstream view is that no hidden-variable completion is required. What Weinstein establishes 
is narrower and stronger for being narrow: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 in these fourteen cases}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 
, the outcomes were fixed by the condition of the systems, and the appearance of a 6.7% incidence rate was an artefact of counting a population instead of reading it. That is a claim about fourteen files, not about God.
\par One further shadow, which the sequel should keep in reserve: the only remaining loophole in Bell tests is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313 superdeterminism}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid4160313  \emdash 
 the possibility that measurement settings and system properties share a common prior cause. It is widely regarded as unmotivated. It becomes considerably less unmotivated in a story where something can adjust the vacuum across a planet for six hours.

