The Same Holiday, Described Twice
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 
The letter had been in the department's general correspondence tray for five weeks, because it was handwritten, and because it began }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 Dear Sir or Madam, I am a travel agent}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 , and because the graduate on general correspondence had a system for handwritten letters which was to put them at the bottom.
\par Neville only found it because he had started reading the general tray himself on Sunday mornings, having concluded in March that the previous administration's chief failure had not been malice but sorting.
\par The letter said:
\par Dear Sir or Madam,
\par I am a travel agent. I have been a travel agent for twenty-two years, at Kind & Farrow of Croydon, which was my father's before it was mine.
\par I am writing about your Statement of Coherence for the period ending 31 March, which I obtained from the Stationery Office, and about paragraph 14, in which you state that the fourteen anomalous resolutions "moved to a state outside the permitted options"
.
\par I do not think anybody moved.
\par I think somebody altered the description, and I think the description is the thing, and I think this is not a philosophical remark. I enclose eleven pages. They are not written in the correct notation and I apologise for that. I did not go to university.

\par If the eleven pages are wrong I should be grateful to be told why, as I have been unable to find anybody to tell me, and I have been carrying it about for four years and it has begun to affect my sleep.
\par Yours faithfully,
\par Susie Kind (Mrs)
\par The eleven pages were in biro. They had been photocopied so many times that the later ones had grey fog along the gutter.
\par Neville read them once at the kitchen table on Sunday and then again standing up, and then he telephoned Fainrose at a quarter to eight in the morning, which he had done exactly twice before.
\par She said, "Is somebody dead."
\par "How much information can you fit in a room."
\par There was a pause of about four seconds.
\par "Who's asking."
\par "A travel agent in Croydon."
\par "Read me a sentence."
\par Neville read her the fourth page.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 A holiday is not a place. It is a set of relations \emdash 
 what you see from the window, what time the light comes, how long it takes to reach the water, who is next to you at dinner. I have sold the identical holiday in two different hotels in two different co
untries and had both parties come home and describe the same fortnight. This used to embarrass me. I now think it is the most important thing I know. If two arrangements produce the same set of relations, they are not two arrangements. There is no further
 fact about which one you were in.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 
\par Fainrose said, "Get her in."
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 
