. In my trade that is two people, and it is never one." Mrs Kind picked up the case. "There is the airline, which has the aircraft and does not care who is on it. And there is the client, who wants to be somewhere. And in between there is a person like me
, who knows which boxes on the form the airline actually reads. A thing that can clear two times ten to the fifty-eight Planck areas of the surface of the Earth is an airline. It does not know what Croydon is. It did not select those fourteen out of tende
rness for the thinnest records."
\par "Then who did," said Neville.
\par "Somebody who could read the register," said Mrs Susie Kind. "Somebody who knew which fourteen records in this country had been reduced to a name and two dead branches, because somebody had been }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 publishing that monthly for nineteen months}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid15733567 
." She did up her coat. "I am not accusing anyone in this room. But I have been in a great many meetings where the disaster was arranged by a person who was only providing information, and I should like to say out loud, before I get my train, that your St
atement of Coherence is a picking list."
\par She got to the door and stopped.
\par "You will want the other thing," she said. "You will have worked out from the shape when it will come round again."
\par "The ninth of February," said Miss Pike.
\par "Then you have until the ninth of February to make everybody fat," said Mrs Kind. "That is the only defence I can see and it is not much of one. A record with live options in it is expensive to clear. Give them somewhere real they could still go. Even one
. Especially the ones nobody is counting." She glanced at Neville. "It is a horrible thing to know, isn't it, that the way to protect a person is to make sure they still have a choice, and that we abolished choices for nineteen months because it made the 
monthly return easier to compile."
\par Neville said, "Mrs Kind. Would you come and work here."
\par She thought about it with complete seriousness for perhaps four seconds.
\par "No," she said. "But I will come on Thursdays. You have nobody who reads forms for a living and it shows."
