\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid13851045 
The two hundred and fourth ordinary session was held in a building off the Ringstrasse that had been a bank, and the Counsel sat at a horseshoe table under a ceiling that had been painted for something else entirely.
\par Ronald Dump, President of the Galactic Counsel, arrived eleven minutes late with six people and no papers.
\par He was a large pale man in a good suit, and Neville, who had prepared himself for a monster, found the reality much more upsetting, because the President of the Galactic Counsel was not a monster. He was a man who had discovered, at some point early in a 
long career, that a room will nearly always give way to whoever is prepared to be simplest, and who had never once needed to learn anything else.
\par He said, into the microphone, before the chair had opened the item:
\par "Nine C. This is the health thing. I've read the numbers. Eleven thousand years, no results. We're not paying for that."
\par The chair said that item 9(c) would be taken in order, and the President said that he was taking it now, and it was taken now.
\par The delegate for a signatory Neville could not identify \emdash  the interpretation gave it as }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid13851045 the Fourth Concordance}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid13851045  \emdash  asked whether the Office had modelled the consequences of discontinuation.
\par "We've modelled it," said the President. "It's fine. Nothing's destroyed. That's the science. I didn't say that, the professor said that. Tell them, Max."
\par Tegn\'e9r, seated at the end of the horseshoe with a folder, said that he had been asked a narrow question about unitarity and had answered it accurately, and that he would prefer not to be characterised as endorsing the wider proposal.
\par "He endorses it," said the President.
\par "I do not," said Tegn\'e9r.
\par "He endorses it."
\par And Neville understood, then, something about the mechanism, which he wrote down that night: that the President did not require Tegn\'e9
r's agreement, only his sentence, and that a true sentence severed from the person who made it is the most powerful object in politics, because you cannot correct it without appearing to correct the physics.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid13851045 
