\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 The theorem was the Austrian's, and Stadthof explained it in the briefing room with chalk and, at one point, by singing.
\par "Take any system of reasoning strong enough to do arithmetic," he said. "It has axioms, it has rules, it is precise, and \emdash  this is essential \emdash  it is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 mechanical}
{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 
: you can check whether a proof is a proof without needing taste. Now. The Austrian's trick, and it is the single cleverest thing any human being has done with a pencil, is to make the system talk about itself. You give every statement a number. Statement
s about numbers therefore become statements about statements. And once you can do that, you can build the sentence that ruins everything."
\par He wrote it on the board in plain English:
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 This statement cannot be proved in this system.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 
\par "Now consider," said Stadthof. "If the system proves it, the system has proved something false, and the system is broken. So if the system is sound, it }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 cannot}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366  prove it. But then the statement is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 true}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366  \emdash 
 that is exactly what it says. So there is a true statement the system cannot reach." He put the chalk down. "Every consistent system of that kind has one. You cannot patch it: add the sentence as a new axiom and the new system generates a fresh one. It 
is not a bug in a particular set of rules. It is a property of having rules at all.
\par "And there is a second part, which is the part your Ms Sloan wants. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 No such system can prove its own consistency.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366  A system that announces it is sound is, by that very announcement, unsound. The only bodies that can certify themselves are the broken ones."
\par Beatrix Sloan, who had not spoken for twenty minutes, said: "Say that again, slowly, and let me write it down in the form I will have to say it in Vienna."
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 
