\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 Priya Ramanathan came down from Cheltenham on the Friday with the list, and it was the list that ended the good mood entirely.
\par "Two hundred and six cores within four thousand years of limit in this arm," she said. "I've been classifying them by whether a six-hour vacuum excursion of the observed depth would advance ignition. Yes, no, or insufficient data."
\par "And?"
\par "A hundred and ninety-seven classified. Yes for thirty-one, no for a hundred and sixty-six." She put the sheet down. "Nine unclassified. And I want to be very careful here, because I spent eleven days assuming the nine were a data problem and they are not
 a data problem."
\par Fainrose said, "Explain."
\par "For those nine, whether the core ignites early depends on the gap between the ground state and the first excited state of the coupled system in a particular regime \emdash 
 the spectral gap." Ramanathan looked up. "There is a result, and it is a proper result, not a curiosity, that the spectral gap problem is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 undecidable}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 
. You can write down the Hamiltonian, exactly, with no uncertainty at all, and there is no algorithm which will tell you in finite time whether the system is gapped. It is the halting problem wearing a lab coat. So when I say nine are unclassified, I do n
ot mean I need a better telescope. I mean that the question of whether those nine stars explode eleven thousand years early }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 has no computable answer}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid5398366 , and I could have the complete state of each one written on a card in my hand and be no better off."
\par Stadthof said, into the silence, "Ah."
\par "Mr Stadthof, this is not a good moment for delight."
\par "It is not delight, Dr Ramanathan, it is recognition, and I am sorry that it looks the same on my face." He had gone quite serious. "That is the Austrian's theorem in a star. That is the third strand and I have been claiming for forty-seven years that the
re was a third strand and I would give a great deal to have been wrong."
