Jack Nashville came in on a Tuesday, by appointment, alone, and would not be met at the station.
\par He was seventy-nine. He wore a mackintosh over a cardigan and carried nothing at all, no case, no papers, which Neville found more intimidating than the wheeled document case had been. He shook hands, sat down, looked at the traces on the plasterboard for
 about a minute and a half without speaking, and then said:
\par "You have a commons problem and you think you have a physics problem. You have both. They are the same problem. Nobody has told you that because the people who do the physics do not read the other literature and the people who do the other literature are 
not asked about stars."
\par Fainrose said, "Mr Nashville\emdash "
\par "Jack is fine. I have been Professor and it did not improve anything."
\par "Jack. Before you start. I asked you here because of a paper of yours from nineteen fifty-one which I read as a graduate student, and because Mrs Kind found your name in the Programme's leasehold schedule, and I would like to know which of those two thing
s you came about."
\par "The second," said Jack Nashville. "The first was a small thing I did quickly when I was twenty-one and I did not think about it again for forty years. It is what I am known for and it is not what I am. I was a geometer. I proved that any curved space, ho
wever strangely curved, can be laid down inside a flat one of enough dimensions without stretching or tearing it \emdash 
 that a description with intrinsic curvature is always available as a description sitting inside something plainer." He glanced at the board, w
here Fainrose's box from Chapter Four was still faintly visible under three weeks of overwriting. "I understand from your Statement that you have recently become interested in that. Nobody has ever asked me about it. They ask me about the twenty-seven pag
es."
\par Miss Pike said, "Why did you come about the leases?"
\par "Because I know what is in them," said Nashville, "and because I have known since nineteen fifty-nine, and because for a long period after that I was not in a condition to be believed, and one becomes careful. I spent some years unable to distinguish a si
gnal from a meaning. It is a very specific kind of illness and one thing it teaches you, permanently, is the difference between a pattern that is there and a pattern you have supplied." He folded his hands. "So I have waited fifty-one years for somebody i
n a government building to publish a number I recognised, and in March your Miss Pike published two hundred and nine out of two hundred and nine, and I bought the Statement from the Stationery Office, and here I am."
