\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 The rest of the morning was the hard half, and Nashville did it slowly, with the air of a man who had explained it before to people who had not wanted to hear it.
\par "Consider a game between two players who cannot communicate," he said. "They are separated. Each receives a question \emdash  a bit, say, chosen at random by a referee \emdash 
 and each must answer. They win or lose according to a rule that depends on both questions an
d both answers. They may agree any strategy in advance. They may carry any notes, any tables, any shared random numbers. They simply may not talk once the questions are asked.
\par "There is a best possible classical strategy, and you can compute it, and for the simplest such game it wins seventy-five per cent of the time. That is a theorem. It is not a matter of cleverness. Seventy-five per cent is the ceiling for any pair of playe
rs who share only ordinary information, however much of it, however cunningly arranged.
\par "Now give them a pair of correlated particles beforehand. Nothing else changes. They still cannot communicate \emdash  and I want to be exact, because this is where everybody goes wrong: they }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 cannot}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 
 signal, not one bit, the correlation transmits nothing, you could not send a message with it if your life depended on it." He paused. "And they win eighty-five per cent of the time."
\par Stadthof, who had come in on his Thursday and stayed for a Tuesday, said, "Which is your Book One number."
\par "It is Bellew's number wearing different clothes," said Fainrose. "Two point eight against a ceiling of two."
\par "Yes. Same content, different presentation, and the presentation matters enormously for what I am about to tell you." Nashville leaned forward. "Because in the version where it is a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 game}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 , you can see immediately what it is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 for}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 
. It is not a curiosity about correlations. It is a mechanism that lets separated parties who cannot communicate, and who do not trust one another, achieve a degree of coordination that is provably beyond anything they could arrange by agreement.
\par "And there are games where the gap is total. There is one \emdash  a small thing with a three-by-three grid and some parity conditions \emdash  where the best classical play wins eight times in nine and cannot do better, and shared correlation wins }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 every single time}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 
. Perfectly. With certainty. Two parties who cannot speak, filling in a grid consistently, always, and no possible arrangement of notes and dice achieves it."
\par Nobody said anything for a while.
\par Neville said, slowly, "You can build compliance out of that."
\par "You can build }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 verified}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384  
compliance out of that, which is much better," said Nashville. "Here is the design and it is not mine, it is eleven thousand years old and I merely recognised it. Each signatory holds correlated partners with the common store. Compliance is not asserted i
n a return; it is demonstrated by playing. A member who has maintained subscription can win the game at the quantum rate. A member who has quietly stopped, and whose held correlations have decayed, cannot \emdash 
 he drops to seventy-five per cent, and he cannot fake his way up, because the ceiling is a theorem and not a standard.
\par "No inspectors. No army. No address. Defection is not punished, it is simply }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 visible}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 
, and it is visible without anybody having to be trusted, and that is why forty-one parties who have never met and do not like each other paid a subscription for eleven thousand years." He sat back. "They were not being good. They were playing a game in w
hich the honest move was the only one that scored."
\par Fainrose had gone very still.
\par "And in June," she said, "the equipment was transferred to the Office of the President."
\par "The equipment, and the map, and the eleven leases," said Mrs Kind.
\par "And the service charge," said Nashville. "Which is the line item that pays for }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 replenishment as required}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 
. Correlations decay, Dr Fainrose. They are not furniture. That is what the money was for. A body which no longer replenishes them is not economising. It is dismantling the only enforcement mechanism in the galaxy, one leased room at a time, on a maintena
nce schedule, without a single vote."
