\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 Beatrix Sloan said, at some point after lunch, "Then I stop drafting."
\par "I beg your pardon?" said Neville.
\par "I have been drafting a treaty since March, on Stephanie's instruction, and I have been doing it in the only way I know, which is words and a forum and a remedy." She had her hands flat on the table again. "It cannot work. There is no bailiff. Jack is rig
ht and he was right in nineteen fifty-nine and nobody listened, and I have wasted five months producing an instrument whose entire operation depends on a body that Mr Stadthof has just proved cannot decide anything."
\par "So what do you draft instead?"
\par "A protocol," said Beatrix. "Not a treaty. Nobody promises anything, because promises need enforcing. Parties simply hold correlated partners and play, at intervals, and the score is the compliance record, and there is nothing to argue about, and nothing 
to interpret, and no Office determines the scope of anything." She looked at Nashville. "Tell me the catch. There is always a catch and you have known it for fifty-one years."
\par "There are two," said Jack Nashville. "The first is monogamy. You cannot hold maximal correlation with two parties at once \emdash 
 your own schedule to the Act says so and it is the reason your S came out at two point four one. So a protocol of this kind has a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 membership limit}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 
. It is not a question of drafting. It is arithmetic. The eleven rooms hold what they hold, and if you spread the store across forty-one members you get forty-one weak correlations, none of which will clear the classical bound reliably, and the whole thin
g collapses into a system of assertions. Which is to say, into a treaty."
\par Beatrix said, "How many can it carry?"
\par "With what is in those rooms, at present decay, and assuming no replenishment after February?" Nashville considered it for perhaps four seconds. "Nine. Possibly eleven if you are willing to accept a scoring interval of a century."
\par "Nine," said Fainrose. "Out of forty-one."
\par "Nine," said Nashville. "And you will have to choose them, and every one you leave out will correctly perceive that you have built a small trusted club out of the wreckage of a universal fund, and they will not be wrong, and I have no advice about that at
 all. I do arithmetic."
\par Neville said, "And the second catch?"
\par And Jack Nashville, who had not raised his voice all day, said:
\par "The second catch is that these games have no optimal play."
