\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 He explained it standing up, at the window, with his back to them, in the manner of a man who does not like the sentence.
\par "For fifty years everybody assumed that any such game has a best achievable score, and that with enough patience you could compute it \emdash 
 approximate it as closely as you liked from above and below and squeeze it. Two independent traditions defined that score in two reasonable ways, and everybody assumed the two definitions agreed, and it was a well-known open problem to prove it.
\par "They do not agree. And the reason they do not agree is that approximating the optimal score of a game of this kind is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 exactly as hard as the halting problem}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 . Mr Stadthof's Austrian, again, at the very bottom of it. You can write down a game \emdash  the questions, the rules, the scoring, all finite, all explicit, a page of paper \emdash 
 and there is no algorithm which will tell you how well it can be played."
\par Stadthof said, very quietly, "Oh, that is beautiful and I hate it."
\par "It gets worse and I want the department to have it," said Nashville, turning round. "It also means that for some games, the score keeps improving as you allow the players larger and larger shared systems, and never settles. There is no finite amount of c
orrelation that is enough. The best play is a limit that is never attained, and the sequence approaching it is not computable, so you cannot even bound how much you are missing." He came back to the table. "For your protocol this has one practical consequ
ence and it is not a good one. You can specify a game whose quantum score you can }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 verify locally}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384  
\emdash 
 the grid game is fine, the score is one, it is certain, it is checkable. But if any party is permitted to propose the game, then you have a mechanism in which a sufficiently patient adversary can put forward a game whose achievable score nobody can com
pute, and demand that the protocol resolve it, and it will hang. For ever."
\par Beatrix closed her notebook.
\par "He will do that," she said. "Within a year."
\par "He will do it in October," said Nashville, "and he will not have understood any of it, and somebody in his office will have read your protocol and mine and worked out that a body which cannot decide is a body which cannot decide }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 against him}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 , and it is the same weapon you used in Vienna, Ms Sloan, and you handed him the drawings when you filed it."

