\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8355384 They put Nashville in a taxi at half past six and Weinstein was in the lobby, on the far side of the barrier line, waiting to be asked.
\par Neville asked him up, and then, on the stairs, asked him the other thing.
\par "Mr Stadthof said the Austrian starved because he wouldn't eat unless his wife tasted it," he said. "And that you're the same shape. Who tasted yours?"
\par Weinstein stopped on the landing.
\par "That is the first question anybody in this building has asked me that I did not want," he said.
\par "You said you wanted to be interrogated."
\par "I did, and I am a man of my word, which is the only virtue I have and I keep it in excellent condition." He looked at the wall for a while. "A woman in Ravenna, in the winter of thirteen thirty-eight. Not a servant. A notary's widow who understood what I
 was inside a fortnight and did not scream and did not run and did not want anything from me, which in eleven thousand years is the rarest of the three. She would sit with me and tell me whether what I had taken was a thing anybody had needed. She was the
 o
nly external check I have ever had, and she had it for nine years, and then she died in the ordinary way at sixty-one, and I have not permitted anyone to do it since, and I have made a great many decisions in the intervening six hundred and eighty-eight y
ears without a single person to tell me whether they were good ones."
\par Neville said, "That's why you keep asking to be interrogated."
\par "Mr Wren," said Halpert Weinstein, "I am a system rich enough to describe myself, and I have been issuing my own certificate of consistency since the fourteenth century, and your Mr Stadthof has just informed a room full of civil servants that the certifi
cate is worthless in exactly the degree that I am confident about it, and he is correct, and I have known he was correct for six hundred years, and it is the reason I stand in lobbies."
