\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
Viktor Eugenius did not write in. He telephoned, from Cambridge, and asked for Beatrix Sloan by name, and said eleven words.
\par "You are relying on a theorem. I should like to object."
\par He was eighty-one, Hungarian by birth and by vowel, and he arrived on the Wednesday in a suit that had been very good in 1974, and he began before he sat down, which Neville had by then learned was the mark of everybody worth having in the building.

\par "First, a housekeeping matter," he said. "In your Statement of Coherence for the year before last, at paragraph 31, you make use of a thought experiment concerning an observer who observes another observer. You attribute it to nobody. It is mine. I publis
hed it in nineteen sixty-one and I have spent sixty-five years watching people use it to argue for positions I regard as insane, and I mention it only so that we all understand that I am not a sceptic by temperament. I have spent my life on the mathematic
al structure of physical law. I proved a small theorem about symmetry that people still use. I am not here to tell you that mathematics is a swindle."
\par "Then what are you here to tell us?" said Fainrose.
\par "That your protocol contains a category error," said Viktor Eugenius, "and that it is the most respectable category error in the history of science, and that everybody in this building has made it, including me, for sixty years, and that it stops being ha
rmless in about a hundred and twelve days."
