\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 He began, unexpectedly, by attacking his own side.
\par "There is a famous essay," he said, "written by a man of my generation, on what he called the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 unreasonable effectiveness}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496  
of mathematics in the natural sciences. His point was a genuine astonishment. A man invents a piece of pure mathematics for reasons of internal beauty, with no application in mind and often with contempt for application. Fifty years later it turns out to 
be the exact language of some physical phenomenon nobody had discovered when he was writing. Complex numbers, invented to handle roots of polynomials, turn out to be the arithmetic of probability amplitudes. Curved geometry, developed as a pure exercise, 
turns out to be gravitation. Group theory, invented for equations, turns out to be why particles come in the families they come in. The essay says: this is a miracle, and we have no right to it, and we should not stop being amazed."
\par "And you disagree," said Miss Pike.
\par "I think it is largely a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 selection effect}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
," said Eugenius, "and I think saying so is the most useful thing an old cosmologist can do in a room like this.
\par "Consider. We do not have a list of every domain of nature and a note of how well mathematics did in each. We have a discipline called physics, and the way a subject gets into it is by }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 proving amenable}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
. Where the mathematics works, we call it physics and give out prizes. Where it does not work, we call it history, or biology before nineteen fifty, or economics, or the behaviour of a committee, and we say that those subjects are }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 not yet mature}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 , which is a remarkable piece of question-begging that has never once been challenged.
\par "Then: we did not find mathematics lying about. We built it, over four thousand years, out of counting sheep and dividing fields and firing projectiles. It would be extraordinary if a tool shaped that thoroughly by the physical world turned out to be no u
se in describing it.
\par "And then the count. Ninety-nine per cent of pure mathematics has no physical application whatever and never will. We remember complex numbers and curved geometry. Nobody writes essays about the enormous graveyard. If you fire ten thousand arrows and elev
en hit the target, and you frame the eleven, you will develop a wonderful theory of your own marksmanship."
\par Stadthof said, "You're taking away my Braid."
\par "I am taking away your }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 miracle}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
, Mr Stadthof, which is not the same thing and you will feel better in a fortnight." Eugenius almost smiled. "A colleague of mine, an engineer, answered the essay properly in nineteen eighty. He said: we see what we look for; we choose the mathematics tha
t fits; science addresses only the tiny fraction of questions it can address; and our brains were shaped by the same physics they are describing. All four are true and all four are dull, and dullness is generally the mark of a correct answer to an over-ex
citing question."
