\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 Wigner's puzzle.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496  
In "The Unreasonable Effectiveness of Mathematics in the Natural Sciences" (1960), Eugene Wigner argued that the applicability of mathematics to physics is a miracle we have no right to expect: concepts developed for internal mathematical reasons repeated
ly turn out to be the precise language of physical law discovered much later. His examples are genuine \emdash 
 complex numbers becoming the arithmetic of amplitudes, Riemannian geometry becoming gravitation, group representation theory becoming the classification of particles, a subject to which Wigner himself contributed decisively.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 The counter-case, which the chapter states fairly.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
 Richard Hamming's 1980 reply, under the same title, offered four partial deflations: we see what we look for; we select the mathematics that fits and discard the rest; science addresses only a small subset of possible questions; and human cognition was i
tself shaped by the physics it describes. To these the chapter adds the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 selection effect}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496  
on disciplines \emdash  membership of "physics" is conferred by mathematical amenability, so the correlation between physics and mathematics is partly definitional \emdash  and the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 survivorship}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
 point that the overwhelming majority of pure mathematics has no physical application and is never counted. This is the direct opposite of the mathematical-universe position that Tegn\'e9
r supplied to the Office of the President, which is why the argument has legal consequences in this book.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 The real technical point: theorems have domains.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
 A mathematical theorem is a conditional statement about a formal structure. Its application to nature depends on the empirical claim that the structure describes the relevant regime \emdash 
 and that claim always has boundaries. This is the ordinary logic of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 effective field theory}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
: every successful theory is valid up to some scale and silent beyond it. Bell-type experiments confirm the quantum bound to great precision, but all of them are performed against an unperturbed vacuum. Extending the bound to a regime where the vacuum str
ucture is itself being modified is an extrapolation, not a deduction.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 The three ceilings, which are all real numbers in the literature.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid8469496 
