Szmulek Barbarian had not held a university post for thirty-one years, and said so in the first minute, in the tone of a man who has decided to get his one embarrassment out of the way before anybody can use it.
\par "I translated Russian physics journals for a living from nineteen sixty-eight until nineteen ninety-three," he said. "Eight hours a day, other men's papers, for a rate per thousand words. It is the best possible training and I recommend it to nobody. You 
read four hundred physicists a year and you notice, eventually, that they are all using a word which none of them defines and which does no work in any of their equations."
\par Fainrose said, "Time."
\par "Time." He sat down. He was seventy-six, very neat, with the stillness of somebody who has spent thirty years thinking in a room without a telephone. "I am going to say something that will make everybody in this room want to be polite to me, and I would r
ather you were rude. I do not think time exists. Not as a scarcity, not as a thing that flows, not as a dimension anything moves along. I think it is what a particular kind of correlation feels like from inside, and I think saying so will be the next revo
lution in physics, and I have thought that for thirty-one years and been wrong about the date every single time."
\par Neville said, "Then let's be rude. My department has a hundred and six days left. What is that, if there's no time?"
