\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 The nine.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 
 Fainrose had to face them on the Thursday, by relay, and she had prepared eleven minutes and used four.
\par "You have asked me to certify direction," she said, "and I am going to refuse, in public, now, rather than in a footnote in eleven years.
\par "The protocol can certify that correlations are held. It can certify redundancy. It can certify that records are mutually consistent. What it cannot do \emdash  not from poor drafting, not from a lack of instruments, but in principle \emdash 
 is tell you which way the galaxy is running.
\par "Because the arrow is not a fundamental fact. It is a gradient in entropy, and a gradient requires a special condition at one end, and we have never explained that condition, we have only assumed it. And the thing our screens were buying was that gradient
. Full subscription is what continuous monitoring looks like from inside, and continuous monitoring is what makes records, and records are what make one direction different from the other.
\par "So here is what I will and will not sign. I will sign that if you hold your correlations and lodge your copies, your compliance is demonstrable. I will not sign that in a volume where screens go slack, your records will continue to distinguish the direct
ion they point in. A weakening arrow does not look like time reversing. It looks like archives becoming ambiguous about which entry came first. It looks like two parties both sincerely remembering being the one who was owed. It looks like eleven thousand 
years of arrangements becoming, very slowly, matters of opinion."
\par The delegate for the Fourth Concordance said, after a pause, "Dr Fainrose, has that been observed?"
\par "Ask me on the tenth of February," said Fainrose.
