\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 Vienna.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635  Nashville went on the Monday, with nothing, and would not be accompanied.
\par He telephoned at twenty past nine on the Tuesday evening and Neville took it standing in the corridor with a pencil and no paper and wrote on the back of his hand.
\par "There are two chairs," said Nashville, "and I have been sitting in one of them for eleven hours and he arrived at four."
\par "He came."
\par "He came at four in the afternoon and he stood in the corridor for forty minutes because nobody had asked him, and I went out and asked him, and he wept, Mr Wren, and I want you to be careful about how you record that, because he is eleven thousand years 
old and he did not do it prettily and he would not want it in a schedule."
\par Neville said, "Did he answer?"
\par "I asked him the question the widow of Ravenna asked him for nine years," said Jack Nashville. "Whether what he had taken was a thing anybody needed. He said that between thirteen forty-seven and two thousand and eight the answer was no, on every occasion
, without exception, and that he had known it at the time and had eaten anyway.
\par "And then I asked him about the seven intervals, because your Miss Pike telegraphed me the plot, and he said \emdash  I wrote this down, and it is the only thing I wrote down all day \emdash 
\par "}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 
Mr Nashville, I did not do it out of remorse. I did it because I am a scavenger and a scavenger who eats the last of anything is a fool. I was maintaining my larder. I would like that understood, because I have been dishonest about a great many small matt
ers and I have never once been dishonest about what I am.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 "
\par "And your verdict?"
\par "My verdict is that it does not matter in the slightest why he did it," said Jack Nashville, "and that is the whole of what an unaffiliated observer is for. I hold no copies in common with any of you and I am not required to like anybody. He put correlati
on into a sealed chamber in Cumbria seven times over fourteen years at his own cost while the Counsel's replenishment line had been dead since nineteen sixty-one, and that is a fact, and I am the only person in the galaxy entitled to say so, and I have sa
id it, and I will sign it in the morning.
\par "And Mr Wren. He asked me a question in return and I could not answer it and I am handing it to you." A pause on the line. "He asked whether, once he had been verified by an outsider, he was still permitted to require an invitation. Because he has stood i
n lobbies for six hundred and eighty-eight years on the grounds that his own certificate is worthless, and if somebody else's certificate is now good, he does not know what he is any more, and he did not ask it as philosophy. He asked it as a man asking a
bout a rule he has organised his entire existence around."
