\af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 \\partial/\\partial t}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 
, no external parameter, and the wavefunction of the universe is stationary. This is the "problem of time" in quantum gravity, and Barbour's position \emdash  presented in The End of Time (1999) \emdash 
 is that it is not a problem but the answer: there exists a timeless configuration space ("Platonia") whose points are relative arrangements of everything, and what we call the passage of time is a property of certain very special configurations, }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 time capsules}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 
, which contain mutually consistent structures interpretable as records of other configurations. On this reading history is not something behind the records; history }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 is}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635  the consistency of the records.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 The mechanism that keeps this respectable.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635  The }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
