 construction (1983) shows how to recover ordinary dynamics from a globally stationary state: partition the system into a clock and a remainder, and consider the remainder's state }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 conditioned}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635  on the clock reading }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 t}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 . That conditional state satisfies the standard Schr\'f6
dinger equation exactly. Time emerges as entanglement between clock and world rather than as a background parameter, and the scheme has been demonstrated in photonic simulations (Moreva et al., 2014). B
arbour's metaphysics is contested; this mechanism is not.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 Why the chapter's rebuttal is the interesting part.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635  
If history is the consistency of records, one might conclude that a past is cheap to manufacture. The department's answer, which is the payoff of Chapter Nine, is that it is not \emdash 
 and the reason is quantitative. A believable history is not one record but a redundancy }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 R_\\delta}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635  
of independent, mutually consistent records held by parties that have not coordinated. Each record consumes surface information capacity at the holographic rate of one bit per four Planck areas (Chapter Four), and the rate is indifferent to whether the re
cord is true. Fabricating a century of history at audit-grade redundancy therefore costs approximately what living it would have cost, plus the substantial overhead of making the copies appear uncoordinated. And it cannot be done on an under-subscribed sc
reen, since slackness is by definition the absence of written capacity. This is why testing redundancy is stronger than testing a score, and it is the chapter's serious point: the reality of the past is underwritten by its expense.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 Why direction cannot be certified.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 
 Fainrose's refusal follows directly. The arrow of time is not intrinsic to t
he dynamics; it is a gradient sustained by a low-entropy condition and, operationally, by the continuous environmental monitoring that produces records at all. Degrade the record-making capacity and the observable consequence is not reversal \emdash 
 reversal is not what a weakening gradient looks like \emdash 
 but the loss of a reliable ordering relation among records. Two archives cease to determine which entry preceded which. In a book about institutions, that is a more frightening prediction than any amount of running backwards.
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0\afs27 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\fs27\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid6510635 
