\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
The paper arrived from the Office of the President on a Tuesday, eleven weeks early, and Jack Nashville, who was still in Vienna in a room with two chairs, was told about it by telephone and said only: "Then he has somebody good. I had assumed he did not.
"
\par There were two parts to it, and the department spent four days on the first and eleven weeks on the second, and got the order wrong, which Neville afterwards recorded as his own error and not Beatrix's.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 Part one}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743  was the game.
\par It was set out with complete propriety on two pages: a certifying game, proposed by a party as the instrument permitted, with the referee's question distribution specified, the winning predicate specified, the scoring interval specified. It was finite. It
 was explicit. It was, as far as anybody could see, entirely regular.
\par Nashville had it read to him down the line, twice, and then said:
\par "That is a very fine object. Whoever built that understood exactly what he was doing. You cannot compute its value and you cannot prove that you cannot, not in the sense of producing a bound \emdash 
 approximating the best achievable score of that game is equiva
lent to deciding whether a machine halts, and the machine they have embedded in it is one nobody has ever settled. It will run for ever and it will look, from the outside, exactly like an audit in progress."
\par Beatrix said, "So we refuse it."
\par "On what ground? The instrument permits any party to propose a game, and the proposal complies in every particular. Refuse it and you have a body determining the scope of its own competence, which is your motion from Vienna aimed at your own head."
\par And then there was a pause on the line, and Beatrix Sloan said, in a voice Neville had not heard from her before:
\par "Jack. We don't test the score any more."
\par "...Say that again."
\par "We rewrote paragraph 11 three weeks ago, on Mr Barbarian's objection. We don't test whether a party can achieve a score. We test the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 redundancy}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743  of their records \emdash 
 how many independent, uncoordinated copies exist, and whether the holders can be shown not to hold copies in common." She was already turning pages. "A proposed game is now evidentially irrelevant. He can propose a game whose value is u
ndecidable and we will say: quite so, and here is the schedule of copies, and the undecidability of your game bears on nothing we rely upon."
\par The line was quiet for a while.
\par "He was eleven weeks early," said Nashville.
\par "He was eleven weeks early."
\par "Ms Sloan, I want you to write down somewhere that the only reason this department survived Tuesday is that a man who denies the existence of time made a pedantic objection to a subordinate clause in June." A dry sound. "Now read me part two, and I am goi
ng to be unhappy, because if the game was the feint then part two is the blow, and they would not have sent both on the same day unless they were confident about it."
