\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 Part two}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743  was four paragraphs and it did not contain a single number.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 The Office notes the amendment to paragraph 11 and does not object to it.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
The Office observes that any volume in which observers are present is necessarily a volume in which conditions have been sufficient for the emergence and persistence of observers. Were subscription in this volume inadequate, the parties now complaining wo
uld not exist to complain. The presence of complainants is therefore itself evidence of adequacy, and the Office invites the session to have regard to it.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
The Office further observes that the parties' own physics establishes that our volume's conditions lie within an exceedingly narrow band, and that no explanation of this fact has ever been offered other than that observers necessarily find themselves in s
uch a band. The Office adopts that reasoning. It does not originate with the Office. It is the settled view of the parties' own literature and has been for fifty years.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 The Office is grateful to Professor M. Tegn\'e9r for his assistance in the preparation of paragraphs 2 and 3.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
