{\*\pnseclvl7\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang {\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl8\pnlcltr\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang {\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}{\*\pnseclvl9\pnlcrm\pnstart1\pnindent720\pnhang {\pntxtb (}{\pntxta )}}\pard\plain \ltrpar
\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 Viktor Eugenius came on the Thursday and read the four paragraphs and put them down and said:
\par "That is the cleverest wicked thing I have read in sixty years, and I want everyone to understand }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 why}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743  it is wicked, because it is not wicked in the way you think. It does not contain a false statement. Again. That is now the third document from that office which is entirely accurate."
\par "Then take it apart," said Neville.
\par "Very well. I shall do the physics first and the swindle second, and the physics is real and everybody should be uncomfortable about it, including me, because I have been uncomfortable about it since nineteen seventy-three."
\par He went to the board, which by August had been overwritten so many times that Fainrose's original box from Chapter Four was a grey ghost under everything.
\par "The constants of nature," he said. "Take the strength of the strong nuclear force. Increase it a few per cent and the diproton binds, and hydrogen fuses catastrophically in the early universe, and there is no long-lived stellar burning of the kind Dr Ram
anathan spent Chapter Five describing. Decrease it a few per cent and deuterium does not bind and there is essentially no chemistry beyond hydrogen.
\par "Take the difference in mass between the neutron and the proton. It is about one part in a thousand of the proton, and if it were slightly smaller the proton would be unstable and there would be no atoms at all, and if it were larger there would be no fre
e neutrons and no heavy elements.
\par "Take the ratio of electromagnetism to gravity, which is about ten to the thirty-six. Change it appreciably and stars are either too short-lived to matter or too cold to fuse.
\par "Take the amplitude of the density ripples in the early universe, which is about one part in a hundred thousand. Smaller and nothing ever gathers into a galaxy. Larger and everything collapses into holes before it can do anything interesting.
\par "And take the worst of them, which is the energy of the vacuum \emdash 
 the same quantity, Dr Ramanathan, that your four instruments measure to eleven parts in ten to the twenty-second. The observed value, in natural units, is around ten to the minus one hundred
 and twenty-two. The naive theoretical estimate is of order one. That is a discrepancy of a hundred and twenty orders of magnitude, and it is the worst quantitative failure of prediction in the history of physics, and nobody has fixed it, and I include th
e string people."
\par Stadthof said, "And the anthropic answer is that we cannot observe a universe we could not exist in."
\par "That is the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 weak}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
 anthropic principle and it is completely uncontroversial and it is not a theory of anything," said Eugenius. "It is a statement about }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 selection of samples}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
, and it is the same logic your department applies to everything: our data are conditioned on our being here to take them. The classic use of it is honourable and I want it on the record because people forget it.
\par "In nineteen fifty-three a man reasoned as follows. There is a great deal of carbon in the universe. Carbon can only be built in stars by three helium nuclei coming together, and the rate at which that happens is far too low unless there is a resonance 
\emdash  
an excited state of carbon-twelve at just the right energy to make the reaction proceed. There is a great deal of carbon; therefore that state exists; therefore it is at approximately seven point six five million electron volts. He told the experimentalis
ts where to look. They looked. It was there.
\par "That is anthropic reasoning at its best. It is a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 prediction}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
, it was risky, it was confirmed, and it worked because the intermediate steps were real physics and the anthropic step was d
oing only one job: telling him which question was worth asking. And there is a second respectable case: in nineteen eighty-seven a physicist argued that if the vacuum energy varies across a large ensemble of regions, observers will find themselves only wh
ere it is small enough to permit galaxies, which puts an upper bound on it \emdash  and he wrote down a value within an order or two of what was then measured eleven years later, before anybody had measured it at all."
\par Beatrix said, "Then the Office's paragraph three is sound?"
\par "Paragraph three is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 sound and empty}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
," said Eugenius, "and here is the swindle, and it is in the joint between paragraph three and paragraph two, and it is done so smoothly that I read it four times before I saw the seam.
\par "Paragraph three says: the constants lie in a narrow band, and observers necessarily find themselves in such a band. True, and it explains nothing whatever about }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 why}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743  the band is where it is, and everybody honest concedes this. It requires an ensemble \emdash  many regions with different values \emdash 
 which the string landscape may supply and may not, and which brings a measure problem nobody has solved: to say a value is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 likely}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
 you need a probability distribution over the ensemble, and there isn't one, and if you choose badly you find that fluctuation-produced observers with false memories outnumber real histories, which is a reductio and not a result.
\par "But paragraph two does something different and it is not physics at all. Paragraph two says: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 since you are here to complain, nothing has gone wrong.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 "
\par He put his finger on the page.
\par "Look at what that does. Anthropic reasoning is a selection effect on }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 observation}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
. It tells you that you cannot use your own existence as evidence about how common your conditions are, because you could not have sampled otherwise. It licenses exactly one conclusion: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 do not be surprised}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 . The Office has converted it into a licence for }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 anything survivable}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
. Every harm short of extinction becomes self-refuting, because anyone left to complain is proof that the conditions permitted complainers.
\par "And observe the direction. It is a bound on the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 worst}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743  
the conditions could have been, not a certificate that they were good. It says the walls cannot have been so slack that nobody survived. Which we knew, madam, because we are all sitting here. It says nothing about two hundred and six cores, or fourteen tu
nnellings, or a hundred and three unrecovered records, and it will nonetheless be reported as science, because the sentence is true and the physicists who might contradict it have spent fifty years arguing about whether the principle means anything and ca
nnot now form a queue."
\par Ramanathan said, "So how do we answer it?"
\par "You do not answer it," said Viktor Eugenius. "You }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 date}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
 it. The anthropic argument is a statement about the conditions that obtained }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 up to now}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
. It has no forward content at all \emdash 
 none, not a syllable, because your survival to the present is consistent with any severity of change beginning tomorrow. The Office's paragraph two is a certificate about the past presented in the tense of a guarantee,
 and if you can make a session of forty-one signatories feel the difference between those two tenses, you will have done more for this galaxy than any of my papers."
\par }\pard \ltrpar\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
