\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 Weinstein's question}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
 was answered on the Saturday, in the afternoon, in the third-floor briefing room, because Beatrix insisted it be done in a room and not in the lobby, and Neville insisted it be done by her and not by him.
\par He had asked whether, having been verified by an unaffiliated observer, he was still permitted to require an invitation.
\par "You are not," said Beatrix Sloan.
\par Weinstein said nothing.
\par "I want to give you the reasoning rather than the ruling, because you are entitled to it and because it is going to be worse than the ruling," she said. "You have believed for six hundred and eighty-eight years that the invitation rule protected you 
\emdash  that it was a discipline you had accepted on the grounds that your own certificate of consistency is worthless, which it is. It was never about you. Schedule four, paragraph nine protects }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 the person whose state it is}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 . Your requirement to be ask
ed is an incident of their right, not a property of your nature. You have organised your entire existence around a rule that was never addressed to you."
\par "I had worked that out," said Weinstein. "Not on the Saturday. In about fourteen hundred and ten."
\par "Then here is the part you had not." Beatrix did not soften it. "Mr Nashville's certificate is good. It has been lodged and it will be relied upon. And its effect is that you now have standing. You are a verified holder of copies, a party of record, a }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 witness}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 . And a witness, Mr Weinstein, may be }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\b\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 summoned}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
. You spent six hundred and eighty-eight years standing in lobbies waiting for somebody to establish externally whether what you did was needed, and you got it in Vienna in eleven hours, and the price of it is that no lobby in this galaxy will ever again 
be a place where you are entitled to wait. You can be compelled now. By us, and by the Office of the President, and by the nine, and by any party with a proper interest, and the compulsion is lawful precisely because you were verified."
\par Halpert Weinstein stood at the window for some time with his back to the room.
\par "You know what I could say," he said eventually.
\par "Yes," said Beatrix.
\par "Say it for me, please. I should like to hear whether it sounds as cheap out loud."
\par "You could say that you are still here after eleven thousand years, and that you have been eating discarded branches for the whole of that period, and that if it had been intolerable the universe would have stopped you, and that your continued existence i
s therefore evidence that your conduct fell within permitted parameters." Beatrix's voice was perfectly level. "It is the Office's paragraph two with a longer run of data. You have a better version of the argument than the President does, because your sam
ple is eleven thousand years and his is four."
\par "It sounds exactly as cheap as I thought," said Weinstein. "And I shall not be using it, and I want that minuted, because I have used every other argument available to a creature in my position and this is the only one I have ever declined." He turned rou
nd. "It is the argument from }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \i\f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 being here}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \f0\kerning0\insrsid14645859\charrsid12780743 
, Ms Sloan, and I have watched it made in nine languages by men standing on the results of a massacre, and it has never once been made by anybody who had a better one available."
\par He picked up his hat.
\par "Summon me," he said. "I have eighty-one days of standing about and I would rather be used. And Mr Wren \emdash 
 the widow in Ravenna asked me the same question in the eleventh year and I lied to her, and she died believing I was permitted, and I have had six hu
ndred and eighty-eight years to consider the ethics of that, and my considered position is that I would do it again and that this is precisely why the rule cannot be left to me."
