\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 Ludger Soccermann had been sixty-two in May of 1906.
\par He had spent thirty years arguing that heat was the statistics of enormous numbers of invisible things, and he had spent those same thirty years being told by extremely eminent men that invisible things were not a proper subject for physics, that his atom
s were a bookkeeping fiction, and that a serious science described what could be observed. He had defended himself very well and in public and it had cost him a great deal. He had been ill for long periods in a way that his century did not know how to nam
e and ours does. He had held chairs in four cities and resigned three of them.
\par He died in September of that year, at Duino, near Trieste, on holiday with his family, by his own hand, at sixty-two, and within four years of his death the atoms were settled beyond argument by other men's experiments.
\par Neville read the biographical note in the registry file and then read the eleven pages in Mrs Kind's translation, and then went and sat in the stairwell again, which by August was where the department went.
\par Because the eleven pages contained the answer to the question they had been failing to answer since Friday, and they contained it fifty-five years before anybody else wrote it down, and they were in a strong room in Vienna the entire time.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
