\s35\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 
\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 What Soccermann had said about entropy}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, in the first four pages, and which Fainrose put on the board on the Thursday because she said the department had been using the word for two books without once earning it:
\par "A quantity of gas in a room," she said. "Describe it. You can say: this pressure, this temperature, this volume. Four numbers. That is the description you can actually obtain, and it is called the macrostate.
\par "Now ask how many distinct arrangements of the individual molecules \emdash  every position, every velocity \emdash  are consistent with those four numbers. Call that number }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs38\f2\fs20\insrsid14645859 W}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 . It is unimaginably large.
\par "Soccermann's claim, and it is the whole of the man, is that entropy is nothing but a count of that number:
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s41\ql \li0\ri0\widctlpar\tx916\tx1832\tx2748\tx3664\tx4580\tx5496\tx6412\tx7328\tx8244\tx9160\tx10076\tx10992\tx11908\tx12824\tx13740\tx14656\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 
\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f2\fs20\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af2 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs38\insrsid14645859 S = k_B \\ln W}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s35\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
"That is it. That is the entire content. Entropy is not a substance, not a fluid, not a tendency. It is the logarithm of how many microscopic arrangements would look identical to you at the level you are able to look.
\par "And once you have that, the second law stops being a law and becomes arithmetic. A system left alone wanders among its available arrangements. There are vastly more arrangements that we would describe as disordered than as ordered \emdash 
 not somewhat more, bu
t more by factors like ten to the ten to the twenty-third. So a system found in an ordered arrangement will, overwhelmingly probably, be found in a disordered one shortly afterwards, for exactly the reason that a shuffled deck is probably not in suit orde
r. Nothing forbids the ordered outcome. It is simply outnumbered."
\par Barbarian, from the corner, said: "And this is where I came in three weeks ago, because the argument runs both ways and he knew it."
\par "He knew it because two people told him," said Fainrose. "One pointed out that if the microscopic laws are reversible, then reversing every velocity gives a lawful evolution in which entropy decreases, so no purely mechanical derivation of the second law 
can exist. The other pointed out that a bounded system must eventually return arbitrarily close to any state it has been in, so entropy cannot increase for ever. Both objections are correct. He accepted both. He was arguing for a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 statistical}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  law and his opponents kept hearing a mechanical one, and he spent the last decade of his life explaining that }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\cs36\i\insrsid14645859 overwhelmingly probable}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  is not the same word as }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 certain}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, to men who took the distinction as a confession of weakness."
\par Miss Pike said, "And was it?"
\par "It was the strongest thing about it," said Fainrose. "Ninety years after he died, people measured it. There are exact results now \emdash  relations that give the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 probability}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  
of a fluctuation in the wrong direction, and they have been confirmed with single colloidal beads in water and with individual molecules of RNA being pulled apart. The second law is violated, briefly, at small scales, at a rate the theory predicts to seve
ral figures. He was right in the only way it is possible to be right about this, and he did not live to see one experiment."
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
