\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The Concordance}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 , and the way out, came on the Tuesday, and it came from van Casteel, who had been silent for a fortnight and had spent it in Cumbria.
\par "You are asking the wrong question about the archive," he said. He had come up on the sleeper and had not shaved. "You keep asking: is an unreadable archive a record or a rumour. That is a librarian's question. I am not a librarian. I have spent nineteen 
years measuring the force between two plates and I want to ask an engineer's question, which is: }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 what can you get out of it?}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 "
\par Beatrix said, "Explain."
\par "Information is not an abstraction in my trade. It is fuel." Van Casteel put his hands flat. "If I hold a system, and I know something about it \emdash  if my memory is correlated with it \emdash 
 then I can extract work from a single heat bath in proportion to what I 
know. That is the demon, and the demon works, and it has been done in a laboratory with a bead and a camera. And there is an exact relation. The extra work you can pull out is bounded by the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 
mutual information}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  between your record and the system, times }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs38\f2\fs20\insrsid14645859 k_B T}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
. No more. Not a joule more.
\par "Now. Suppose I take the Fourth Concordance's archive into my chamber, and I put it against a reference, and I try to extract work. What happens?
\par "If the archive genuinely holds four hundred years of correlations, then whatever I cannot decode, the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 correlations are physically there}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, and there is work in them, and I can measure the work with a torsion balance to eleven significant figures, because that is the only thing I know how to do.
\par "And if the archive is a fabrication \emdash  a set of marks that look like records but are correlated with nothing \emdash  then there is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 no work in it at all}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid14645859 . Not a little less. None. A forged archive is thermodynamically dead. You cannot fake mutual information, because mutual information is not a property of the marks. It is a relation, and a relation you have invented has no engine in it."

\par There was a very long silence in the briefing room.
\par Beatrix Sloan said, slowly, "You can certify the archive without reading it."
\par "I can certify }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 how much is in it}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, and that it is genuinely correlated, and I cannot learn one word of its content, and I regard the second part as a feature," said Hendrik van Casteel. "I do not want to read the Fourth Concordance's four hundred years. It is none of my business. I want 
to weigh it."
\par Fainrose was already at the board.
\par "That's the amendment," she said. "Paragraph 11 does not require readable copies. It requires copies whose mutual information with the certified subject is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 thermodynamically demonstrable}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 . Which the Concordance can satisfy, because their records are real. And which no fabricated archive can satisfy at any price, because\emdash "
\par "Because a forger would have to actually correlate his archive with an actual four hundred years," said Miss Pike, "which is not forgery. That is just doing it."
\par "That is just doing it," said Fainrose.
\par Barbarian said, from the corner, "Then a past is expensive after all, and the price is quoted in joules, and I have been arguing for thirty-one years that history is nothing but the consistency of records and I am now told that the consistency can be }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 weighed}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
." He was rubbing his forehead. "Dr Fainrose, I came here in July to destroy your protocol and I have accidentally underwritten it twice. I should like to go home and think about whether I still believe anything."
