Nelson Drill was eighty-eight and arrived by taxi with a driver he had evidently talked to for the entire journey, since the driver came in to help him with the door and then stayed for eleven minutes listening to the end of something.
\par He had been, in order: a goalkeeper for a provincial Danish club, briefly and without distinction; an apprentice in a firm that made survey theodolites; and then, for thirty-six years, the man who designed test benches for people who needed to measure som
ething nobody had measured before. He had never held a university post. He had never published a paper. He had built approximately four hundred instruments, and he had a way of speaking that Neville was entirely unprepared for.
\par "I should say at once," he said, "or rather \emdash  no. Let me put that differently, because 'at once' suggests I have a position and I do not have a position, I have an }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 objection}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 , which is a smaller thing and I should not inflate it. Or \emdash  well. It is not smaller. It is differently shaped. May I begin again?"
\par "Please," said Fainrose.
\par "I have read your Chapter Three finding eleven times and I believe it is invalid, and I want to be careful, because I do not believe it is }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 wrong}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
." He sat down slowly. "Those are not the same and the difference is the whole of what I have come to say. I think you have reached a conclusion that may well be correct by a route that is not permitted, and in my trade that is worse than an error, becaus
e an error can be found."
