It took the whole morning, and Neville gave up trying to take a verbatim note at eleven o'clock, because Nelson Drill could not produce a sentence that survived being written down. He revised as he went. He would reach the end of a proposition and then sa
y "though that is too strong" and take a piece of it back, and then take back the taking back. Twice he asked whether anybody had a better word than "arrangement" and then rejected all four offered.
\par By half past twelve Miss Pike had stopped writing and started drawing benches, which turned out to be the correct response.
\par "Your finding," said Drill, "rests on two observations. I shall state them as flatly as I can and you will please correct me, because I am about to be unfair to your Mr Weinstein and I should like to be unfair accurately.
\par "Observation one. On the fourteenth of March, fourteen pending states resolved to conditions outside their permitted options. That observation was made by Miss Pike, using the register, the traces, and four instruments. It is an observation made on a }{
\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 bench}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 : a specified arrangement of recording apparatus, with a defined coupling to the systems, producing marks.
\par "Observation two. On the fourteenth of March, there was no discarded amplitude \emdash 
 no waste, no leavings, nothing to eat. That observation was made by a single organism whose coupling to those systems is of a completely different character, which cannot be 
specified, which cannot be repeated, which produces no marks, and which is available to precisely one observer in the galaxy.
\par "Your finding then does something. It says: since observation two shows no waste, and waste is the signature of selection, therefore the outcomes recorded in observation one were determined rather than selected.
\par "And that step, Dr Fainrose, is the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 combination of results from two mutually exclusive arrangements as though they were simultaneous facts about the same systems}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\insrsid14645859 
. And that is not a technicality. That is the specific error which was made in nineteen thirty-five by a very great man in a paper about incompleteness, and it took the rest of us fifty years to be sure it was an error, and I built benches for thirty-six 
years and I can tell you that it is the only error in the subject that never announces itself."
\par Weinstein, who had been summoned and who had been sitting very still at the end of the table, said: "Say the nineteen thirty-five error, please. Properly. I have heard it described nine ways and I have never once heard it from somebody who built the appar
atus."
