\s35\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 Drill did it with two pencils and a matchbox, and it took twenty minutes, and Neville put the reconstruction in the minute afterwards because he could not reproduce the way it was said.
\par "Two particles, correlated, flying apart," said Drill. "I may choose to measure the position of the near one, and I then know the position of the far one, without having gone anywhere near it. Or I may choose to measure the momentum of the near one, and I
 then know the momentum of the far one, equally without going near it.
\par "The argument of nineteen thirty-five says: since I could have predicted either one with certainty, and since I plainly did not disturb the distant particle in either case, }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 both}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  the position and the momentum must have been there all along as elements of reality. And since the theory cannot describe both at once, the theory is incomplete.
\par "Now." He put down the pencils. "The reply \emdash 
 and it was published in the same journal within months, and I am afraid it was published rather badly, in prose that has caused a hundred years of trouble, and I say that as someone who spent his life admiring the man \emdash 
 the reply is that the argument contains an ambiguity in the phrase }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 without in any way disturbing}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 .
\par "There is no mechanical disturbance. Everyone agrees. Nothing is jogged. But choosing which measurement to make on the near particle }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 defines what kind of prediction is available}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  
about the far one. It fixes the conditions under which a claim about the far particle has any meaning. And you cannot help yourself to the conditions of two mutually exclusive arrangements and then complain that no single description contains both, becaus
e the two descriptions are not two accounts of one situation. They are accounts of two situations, and you have to build one bench or the other."
\par "That is complementarity," said Fainrose.
\par "That is complementarity, and I want to say a hard thing about it in this room, because I have read your Statement of Coherence for the year before last and I know what this department did in Chapter Five." Drill looked around. "Complementarity, as it was
 meant, is not a theory of black holes. It is a rule about }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 benches}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
. It says: position and momentum, or wave behaviour and which-path behaviour, require arrangements that are mutually exclusive as a matter of hardware, and the phenomenon is not the particle, it is the particle }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\cs36\i\insrsid14645859 and the instrument}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 , and you may not carve the instrument off and call the remainder a property.
\par "Thirty years ago some very good people took the word and applied it to a horizon \emdash 
 the claim that the account from outside and the account from within are both complete and may not be combined. That is a much stronger and much more speculative conjecture
, and your Ms Sloan destroyed it in Vienna with the monogamy theorem, and she was entirely right to.
\par "But you then came home believing you had disposed of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 mine}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, and you had not. You had disposed of a cousin of mine who wears my name badly. My rule is about apparatus, and it has never been refuted, and in nineteen sixty-seven it acquired a theorem, and I shall come to the theorem after lunch because I am eighty-
eight and I should like some soup."
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
