The nine sent no letter this time. They sent a man, on the Monday, unannounced, who gave his name to the desk as "Esaki, retired," and who was shown up to the sixth floor by a junior clerk who had no idea that the visitor had spent the 1950s doing somethi
ng to a p-n junction that the rest of physics took thirty years to believe.
\par He was ninety-one. He sat down, declined tea, and said, before anyone had asked him a single question:
\par "You are troubled by the fourteen because they went where the barrier said they could not go. I built a career on the fact that this is not troubling. It is Tuesday."
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
