\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 He took a pencil \emdash  Drill's, left behind on the table, which Ramanathan had kept out of what Neville suspected was sentiment \emdash 
 and drew a rectangle, and then a wavy line running into it and, faintly, out the other side.
\par "Here is what you keep getting backwards," he said. "You keep asking: how did the fourteen get through when they had no energy to get through? As though the barrier is an obstacle and the particle is a traveller and the traveller has cheated. That is a st
ory about traffic. It is not the physics.
\par "The particle is not a traveller. It is a wave. A wave incident on a barrier does not stop dead at the wall the way a ball stops at a wall. Its amplitude decays }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 inside}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  the barrier \emdash  exponentially, but not to zero \emdash 
 and on the far side, if the barrier is not too thick, there is a non-zero amplitude still standing. That amplitude means a non-zero probability of finding the particle over there, having never possessed the energy to be over the top.
\par "It has not cheated a toll. It has not borrowed energy and failed to pay it back, though I have read that phrasing in three of your reports and I want it struck from every one of them, because it invites exactly the question you keep failing to answer, wh
ich is 'borrowed from where and paid back when', and there is no loan and there is no ledger entry and no lender. The amplitude was simply there, on both sides, the entire time, as the solution to one equation with one barrier in it. Nothing travelled thr
ough the classically forbidden region carrying anything. The wave was already on both sides before you asked which side it was on."
\par Barbarian, predictably, said: "Then in what sense did it cross a barrier at all?"
\par "In the sense that matters to a fabrication engineer," said Esaki, "which is the only sense I have ever cared about. Current flowed on the wrong side of a junction that classical electron transport says should have been dark. I did not need to know what t
he electron 'felt' crossing. I needed a diode that worked at frequencies nothing else could reach, and I built it, and it is in every satellite receiver launched for forty years, and the metaphysics of the crossing was never once relevant to whether the t
hing switched in picoseconds."
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
