\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The profound remark}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  \emdash  which is what the department, afterward, insisted on calling it, over Esaki's objections that it was merely correct \emdash 
 came at half past three, after Fainrose had spent an hour trying to get him to comment on Chapter One's finding about the fourteen pending states.
\par "You want to know," he said, "whether the barrier the fourteen crossed on the fourteenth of March was a barrier of the same kind as mine. A junction in a crystal. A specific, local, measurable thing, thin enough in one direction for an amplitude to surviv
e the crossing.
\par "I will tell you what I told the Swedish committee in nineteen seventy-three, which they did not put in the citation because it did not sound like physics, though I have never said anything I believed to be truer.
\par "A barrier is not a wall. A barrier is a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 region where the classical description says the amplitude ought to be zero, and it is not.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  
That is the entire definition, and it does not care what the barrier is made of. It does not care whether it is a depletion region a few nanometres wide, or a mountain of potential energy in a nucleus deciding whether to undergo alpha decay, or \emdash 
" he paused, and looked directly at Fainrose \emdash 
 "a permitted option in a register, if your register is in fact a description of amplitude and not, as I gather from young Mr Drill's report, which reached me before I came here, a description of anything at all.
\par "So here is the sentence, since you keep asking for one. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 
Every barrier in this universe that has ever been crossed by something that should not have had the energy to cross it was crossed the same way: not by having enough, but by never entirely being on one side of it to begin with.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  I built my career on a junction three nanometres wide. Your fourteen crossed a barrier of an entirely different construction, and if it is a real barrier \emdash  a real amplitude, a real forbidden region \emdash 
 then they crossed it for the same reason my electrons d
id, and it will not matter one particle's worth how large or small or strange the barrier is. Barriers are not crossed by force. They are crossed by never having been fully excluded from the other side in the first place."
\par There was a silence that Neville, writing it up later, described as "the kind the room gives you when it has just been corrected by someone who is not trying to win."
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sl259\slmult1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
