\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 Chapter Fourteen carries }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 quantum tunnelling}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
 as Esaki actually built it: the resolution of the "how did it get the energy" intuition, and the reframing of a barrier as a property of amplitude rather than geography.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The Esaki (tunnel) diode.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  Leo Esaki, working at Sony (then Tokyo Tsushin Kogyo) in 1957, discovered that in an extremely heavily doped p-n junction 
\emdash  with a depletion region only a few nanometres wide \emdash  current flows under reverse bias at voltages too small to explain by thermionic or avalanche mechanisms. He correctly identified the cause as }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\cs37\b\insrsid14645859 quantum mechanical tunnelling}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  
of electrons through the narrow potential barrier of the junction, work for which he shared the 1973 Nobel Prize in Physics with Ivar Giaever and Brian Josephson. Tunnel diodes remain in use for high-frequency oscillators and detectors precisely because t
unnelling is a fast, direct quantum process with no classical transit time in the barrier region.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Why tunnelling is not "borrowing energy."}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  A common but misleading gloss on tunnelling invokes the energy\endash 
time uncertainty relation to suggest a particle "borrows" energy to surmount a barrier and "pays it back" afterward. This is not the accepted mechanism and the chapter is right to have Esaki r
eject it. The correct picture, from solving the time-independent Schr\'f6
dinger equation for a particle incident on a finite potential barrier, is that the wavefunction does not vanish at the barrier wall. Inside a classically forbidden region (where the poten
tial exceeds the particle's total energy), the wavefunction decays exponentially rather than oscillating, but it does not reach zero if the barrier is finite in width. If the barrier ends while the amplitude is still non-zero, the wavefunction resumes osc
illating on the far side with reduced but non-zero amplitude \emdash 
 giving a non-zero transmission probability. The particle's total energy never exceeds the barrier height at any point; there is no loan, because there is no classical trajectory to which energy 
conservation, moment to moment, needs to be applied. The amplitude simply already extends across both sides as the single, self-consistent solution to one equation.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The generality Esaki insists on.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  The same mathematics governs:
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid14645859 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}\pard\plain \ltrpar\ql \fi-360\li720\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar
\jclisttab\tx720\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\ls24\adjustright\rin0\lin720\itap0\pararsid5709733 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31507\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 \f31506\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\kerning2\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 
\ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Alpha decay}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
 (Gamow, 1928): a nucleus's alpha particle tunnels through the Coulomb barrier, with tunnelling probability exponentially sensitive to barrier width and height \emdash  explaining the enormous range of observed half-lives from a single mechanism.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid14645859 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Scanning tunnelling microscopy}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  (Binnig and Rohrer, 1981): a vacuum gap between tip and surface is the barrier; exponential sensitivity of tunnelling current to gap width gives sub-angstrom resolution.
\par {\listtext\pard\plain\ltrpar \rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\f3\fs20\kerning2\insrsid14645859 \loch\af3\dbch\af31506\hich\f3 \'b7\tab}}{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Nuclear fusion in stellar cores}{\rtlch\fcs1 
\af31507 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
 (as established in Chapter Five's fictional register): fusing nuclei tunnel through their mutual Coulomb repulsion at energies far below the classical threshold, which is why stars fuse at all at core temperatures that classical physics says should be fa
r too cold.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s35\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The reframing.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  The chapter's central move \emdash 
 voiced through Esaki and sharpened by Mrs Kind's travel-agent analogy \emdash  is that a "barrier" is not a substance or a geographic obstacle but a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 
statement about where a classical description predicts zero amplitude}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
. Whether that statement is true is an empirical question about the actual wavefunction, not a metaphysical one about the particle's virtue or cunning. Applied reflexively to the book's plot: whether the department's "permitted options" register describes
 a genuine forbidden amplitude, or merely marks the edge of what the register's own instruments could detect, is exactly the kind of question Chapter Thirteen's contextuality result says cannot be settled without characterising the instrument. Esaki's tun
nel diode is offered as the clean case where the barrier was known independently (crystal geometry, doping profile) and the amplitude calculation could be checked against measured current \emdash 
 a luxury the department, per Drill, has not yet earned for its own register.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s3\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sb160\sa80\sl259\slmult1\keep\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel2\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31503\afs28\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
