\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 Fainrose read it aloud to the department on the Tuesday morning, and her voice did something to the second page that Neville had never heard it do before.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 My solution has a curious feature which I set aside from my note to Einstein because it did not seem a matter for a paper written from a trench, though I now believe it a matter for exactly that.}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 At the radius where I set my metric's coefficient to zero \emdash  the radius, I am told since, which some of my colleagues are already calling by my name, though I would rather they did not 
\emdash  the mathematics does not stop. It changes character. Time and space e
xchange their roles inside that radius, in a manner I have not the leisure remaining to me to state carefully, and the equations, extended, describe not a single region collapsing inward, but two regions, joined at a throat, neither of which is the other,
 connected by a passage which the mathematics permits and which no matter travelling forward in time may ever cross back through.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 
I do not know what is at the other end. I strongly suspect nothing is, in the sense of there being no matter that put it there; I suspect it is a property of the equations themselves, the way a corridor is a property of a building's plan before anyone wal
ks down it. But I have solved the equations for a single point mass, alone in an otherwise empty universe, and the geometry I obtain is not one region with a dense centre. It is two.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 
I mention this to your office rather than to the Berlin physicists for the following reason. If two regions of an otherwise unconnected universe can be joined by a throat that the equations permit but which no traveller may ever use twice, then the questi
on of what lies "outside" a thing is a question your office presumably already has cause to ask of stranger objects than mine. I am a lieutenant of artillery and I do not expect to see the end of this war. I should like this observation to survive me, in 
case it is of use to whoever eventually has to explain how two things came to be joined without either of them having travelled to reach the other.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
