\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The profound remark}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, when it came, was not from a physicist at all, and Neville was careful to record that it came from Weinstein, because everybody afterward tried to attribute it to Ramanathan and it was not hers.
\par "May I say the thing your lieutenant could not finish," Weinstein said, "because he ran out of paper, or ran out of life, and either way it is the same interruption."
\par "Please," said Fainrose.
\par "He said he did not know what was at the other end, and suspected it was nothing \emdash 
 a property of the equations, like a corridor in a building's plan before anyone walks down it. He was careful. He was a soldier writing to a government office and he did not want to be thought fanciful.
\par "I have had eleven thousand years to not be careful, and I will say the thing he would not permit himself. A corridor that exists in the plan before anyone walks down it is not nothing. It is the single most important sentence in architecture, and your li
eutenant wrote it from a trench because he had, at that exact moment, more courage than comfort, which is when people write their truest sentences.
\par "A tunnel between two galaxies is not remarkable because something might use it. It is remarkable because the geometry does not care whether anything ever does. Two regions of an otherwise disconnected universe are already joined, in the mathematics, the 
instant the mass exists, whether or not a single particle ever makes the crossing. The bridge is not built when it is used. The bridge is built when the equations are satisfied, and after that, using it is merely a separate and much harder question, and I
 would remind this entire department, which has spent fifteen chapters asking whether things are permitted, that your lieutenant already answered a bigger question first.
\par "He proved that separateness itself is not the default. Two places do not require a history of travel between them to be connected. They require only that the same equation is satisfied at both ends. I have carried the far end of such a bridge on my own b
ack for eleven thousand years and did not know it was a bridge, because I had never been introduced to the man clever enough, and brave enough, and short-lived enough, to tell me what I was standing on."
