\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 Neville wrote, that night:
\par A lieutenant of artillery solved the exact equations by lamplight, between guns, in 1916.\line He sent the tidy half to Einstein. He sent the strange half to us. We did not open it for 109 years.\line 
'Pending confirmation of addressee's survival.' Nobody checked. He did not survive. Nobody checked that either.
\par Then:
\par His solution, honestly extended, is not one region. It is two, joined at a throat, at a single instant.\line The throat pinches shut before anything can cross. A bridge that exists for less time than it takes to use.\line 
Ramanathan: 'the thing his horizon has been doing to my instruments for six months. I called it noise.'
\par Then, underlined twice:
\par Van Casteel: to hold a throat open you need negative energy density.\line Casimir energy is negative. We measured it in a mineshaft for two chapters and filed it under vacuum and moved on.\line 
Something has been propping a throat open behind Weinstein for eleven thousand years\line using the one resource in our own casework with the correct sign.
\par Then, Weinstein's sentence, which Neville copied out twice to be sure he had it exactly:
\par 'The bridge is not built when it is used. The bridge is built when the equations are satisfied.'\line Separateness is not the default. Two places need no history of travel between them.\line They need only that the same equation holds at both ends.\line 
He carried the far end of a bridge for eleven thousand years and did not know, because he had never met the man\line clever enough, and brave enough, and short-lived enough, to tell him what he was standing on.
\par Then:
\par Beatrix: not destroyed, not stored, not forgiven. POSTED.\line An address nobody checked because nobody knew there was another end.
\par And last:
\par Van Casteel is building the bench. Ramanathan wants to know if the throat is traversable\line or only technically true.
\par Karl Schwarzschild never saw the end of his war. I would like, once, to finish something\line before the paper runs out.
\par 68 days. Thirty-one cores still advancing. I still have not asked what that costs us.
\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s2\ql \fi-14\li144\ri0\sb160\sa80\sl259\slmult1\keep\keepn\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\outlinelevel1\adjustright\rin0\lin144\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af31503\afs32\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
