\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 Chapter Fifteen carries the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 maximally extended Schwarzschild solution}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  and the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Einstein\endash Rosen bridge}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 : general relativity's own wormhole, its real history, and why it is not \emdash 
 on its own \emdash  a useful tunnel.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Schwarzschild's actual history.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  
Karl Schwarzschild, an astronomer serving as a German artillery lieutenant on the Eastern Front, found the first exact solution to Einstein's field equations in December 1915, for the spacetime around a single non-rotating point mass \emdash 
 remarkable because 
Einstein himself had only produced an approximate solution weeks earlier. Schwarzschild sent his paper to Einstein, who presented it to the Prussian Academy of Sciences on his behalf. Schwarzschild died in May 1916 of pemphigus, an autoimmune disease, con
tracted at the front, having continued working on relativity between military duties until close to the end. The chapter's device \emdash  a second, unsent portion of the correspondence \emdash 
 is invention; the man, the timeline, the disease, and the astonishing speed of the result are historical.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 What the solution actually contains.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
 The Schwarzschild metric, in its original coordinates, appears to have a singularity at the Schwarzschild radius }{\rtlch\fcs1 \af2\afs20 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs38\f2\fs20\insrsid14645859 r_s = 2GM/c^2}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
. This is a coordinate artefact, not a physical one \emdash  resolved by better coordinate choices (Eddington\endash Finkelstein, and fully by Kruskal and Szekeres independently in 1960). The }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 
maximally extended}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  solution reveals the true global structure: two separate asymptotically flat regions, connected by a throat, joined at a single moment of symmetry. This is the }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Einstein\endash Rosen bridge}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, named for Einstein and Nathan Rosen's 1935 paper recognising the same structure while attempting (unsuccessfully) to model particles as bridges. It is popularly called a wormhole.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Why it cannot be used.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  The Kruskal extension shows the throat pinching closed in a finite proper time, faster than any signal \emdash 
 let alone any traveller \emdash  could cross from one side to the other. The classical, uncharged, non-rotating Einstein\endash Rosen bridge is a genuine feature of
 the solved equations and is, simultaneously, provably non-traversable: it closes before anything gets through. This has been understood since Fuller and Wheeler's 1962 analysis.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Traversability and exotic matter.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  Morris and Thorne (1988), motivated by a query from Carl Sagan for the novel }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\cs36\i\insrsid14645859 Contact}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 , showed what is required to hold a wormhole throat open long enough to be crossed: a source of }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 
negative energy density}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 , violating the null energy condition that ordinary matter obeys. This is not a free assumption \emdash  it demands a real physical mechanism. The }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 
\cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Casimir effect}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, the attractive force between closely spaced conducting plates arising from the modification of vacuum fluctuations, is the best-established real-world source of a negative energy density (already present in the story via Chapter Two and Hendrik van Cast
eel). The magnitudes achieved in laboratory Casimir setups are vastly too small to hold open anything but a subatomic wormhole for an imperceptible time, which is precisely the gap between "the correct sign exists in nature" and "a usable macroscopic brid
ge
 exists" that the chapter turns into its central tension. Traversable wormholes remain, at present, solidly theoretical: consistent with general relativity given exotic matter, but with no known astrophysical source of exotic matter at the required scale.

\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Why the plot's leap is the right kind of speculative.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  
Treating an eleven-millennia-old, continuously fed, never-evaporating horizon as a candidate site where the ordinary Fuller\endash 
Wheeler pinch-off might have been indefinitely deferred by a sustained negative-energy source is invention, not established physics \emdash 
 but it is invention built from real, correctly cited pieces (the maximal extension, the Casimir sign, the traversability condition) rather than from nothing, which is the standard the book holds itself to throughout.
