\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Drill's instrument}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, invoked one last time, closed the liability question, and it was Beatrix who did it, on the Thursday, in front of the nine's own representative, who had come in person for the first time in the book's history.
\par "Mr Drill taught this department that an uncharacterised instrument is not evidence, it is a lead," she said. "Dr van Casteel has now characterised the throat. Dr Ramanathan has characterised the flow through it. Between them, Mr Weinstein is, for the fir
st time in eleven thousand years, a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 calibrated apparatus}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  \emdash 
 coupled to a measured cross-section, discharging into a measured aperture, at a measured rate, into a destination we cannot see but can now bound.
\par "That changes the schedule I proposed in Chapter Thirteen. I was going to advise the Crown to inherit an undischargeable ledger with no floor to it \emdash 
 an infinite liability, because a debt with no mechanism of discharge is, in law, exactly that. It is not 
that any longer. The debt discharges. It has been discharging for eleven thousand years, continuously, through a channel we can now measure, at a rate we can now write into a schedule.
\par "Mr Weinstein is not a bottomless liability. He is a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 very slow drain}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 , attached to a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 
very old pipe}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, and the Crown can insure a drain. What the Crown cannot insure is a wall with nothing behind it, which is what I believed I was looking at in August, and I was wrong, and I would rather be wrong in this direction than any other I can think of."
\par The nine's representative \emdash  a small, precise woman named Okonkwo-Barrett who said almost nothing throughout and whom Neville never saw again \emdash 
 asked one question only: "Is the drain adequate to the current caseload, going forward, or does the department require a second Mr Weinstein?"
\par "God, no," said Weinstein, before anyone else could answer, and it was the first time in the whole book he had used the word without apparent irony, and nobody remarked on it, though Neville wrote it down twice.
