\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The nine undecidable cores}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 , promised at the end of Chapter Fourteen, were resolved \emdash  or rather, were shown to require no resolution \emdash 
 by Fainrose, on the Friday, and it took her four minutes and it undid a worry the department had been carrying since Chapter Six.
\par "I went back and checked what the nine undecidable cores from the G\'f6
del-sentence motion actually have in common," she said, "because Drill's lesson was that a shared property nobody checked for is exactly the kind of thing this department misses. And they
 have one, and it is embarrassingly simple, and I am furious it took this long.
\par "All nine sit within the measured cross-section of van Casteel's throat. Not near it. Not associatively connected to it by some poetic resemblance. Within the actual, measured, physical volume through which the slow drain passes, on its way to wherever it
 is going.
\par "They were never undecidable because the mathematics failed. They were undecidable because the register was trying to certify a yes-or-no answer about objects that are, right now, partly }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 there}{
\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  and partly wherever the throat's far end is \emdash  which is precisely the kind of statement Beatrix's G\'f6
del-sentence motion predicted a system cannot certify about itself from the inside. The nine aren't a defect in our arithmetic. They're a }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 list of everything currently in transit}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 
\ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 . Mavis-9 declined to certify herself in Chapter Six for the same reason a customs officer declines to stamp a passenger currently airborne. Not lost. Not contraband. }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ai\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs36\i\insrsid14645859 
In transit,}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  and the paperwork simply has no box for that, and shouldn't be made to grow one."
\par Mrs Kell, eighty-three, who had sat through the whole meeting in silence, said: "Then file them as in transit. Don't force the box. We are not the last people who will ever look at this register, and the next people will thank us for leaving the question 
open rather than for closing it wrong out of impatience."
\par The motion passed the departmental panel eleven votes to nothing, with Mrs Kell herself abstaining, on the grounds \emdash  minuted verbatim \emdash  that "a person who proposes the sensible answer should not also get to vote for it."
