\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Weinstein's clause}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
, the last piece of the book's business, was Weinstein's own idea, and he brought it to Beatrix rather than waiting to be summoned, which nobody in the department had ever seen him do.
\par "I should like Article Two amended," he said. "Not the invitation clause \emdash 
 leave that exactly as it stands, it is the only rule in eleven thousand years that has ever actually protected anyone, including, on one occasion I am not proud of, me. I should li
ke a schedule added. A public one. Every discarded branch I decline to keep, from this day, is logged, gated, and dated, the moment before it goes down the throat, so that if anyone \emdash  Nashville, in Vienna, or whoever holds his chair after him 
\emdash  ever finds a way to ask the other end a question, there will be a manifest of exactly what was sent, and when, and why it was let go rather than kept."
\par Beatrix looked at him for a long moment. "That is not a legal instrument. That is a confession, filed in advance, of everything you have not yet done."
\par "Yes," said Weinstein. "Mr Drill told this room that an uncharacterised instrument is not evidence. I have decided I would rather be evidence than a lead, for the remainder of however long I am obliged to exist, and I find I would rather start now than wa
it to be asked again by somebody with a warrant."
\par Neville, watching Beatrix draft the schedule that afternoon, thought \emdash  and did not say, because it was not the kind of thing one said to Beatrix Sloan \emdash 
 that this was the first sentence in either book in which Halpert Weinstein had asked to be held to something before anyone had thought to require it of him.
