\ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The ninth of February}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  arrived on a Monday, cold, with nothing scheduled after eleven, which was itself unprecedented.
\par Neville came in early and found Mrs Kind already at her desk, doing what she had done since Chapter Four: closing out the file the way a travel agent closes out a booking, checking every leg had actually been flown, not merely paid for.
\par "Fourteen tunnelled," she read aloud, for the record, one last time. "Three hundred and eleven of three eleven \emdash 
 no. Three hundred eight recovered, of the original tally, plus the fourteen, accounted a different way. Two hundred nine of two hundred nine d
ipped and returned. Nine cores in transit, correctly logged as such and not otherwise. Thirty-one cores, as of Friday's reading, still advancing \emdash 
 Ramanathan says that number has not moved in six weeks, which she says, for a supernova countdown, is the best
 news she has had all year, because it means the field maintenance the Counsel voted to preserve after all, buried in an amendment nobody minuted properly in Chapter Five, is holding." She looked up. "I don't think anyone ever told you that vote went the 
other way on appeal."
\par "Nobody told me," said Neville.
\par "The Counsel reinstated funding in July. Quietly. Ronald Dump's office issued a one-line correction and hoped nobody would ask why. I only found out because I still had the travel booking open for the delegation that never happened, and I had to cancel a 
hotel."
\par Neville laughed, for the first time in the book, out loud, in the office, which several people noted and none commented on.
