\par }\pard\plain \ltrpar\s35\ql \li0\ri0\sb100\sa100\sbauto1\saauto1\widctlpar\wrapdefault\aspalpha\aspnum\faauto\adjustright\rin0\lin0\itap0\pararsid14645859 \rtlch\fcs1 \af0\afs24\alang1025 \ltrch\fcs0 
\fs24\lang1033\langfe1033\cgrid\langnp1033\langfenp1033 {\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859 
Chapter Sixteen carries no single new concept forward; it is the book's synthesis chapter, and the physics is in how three previously separate threads are shown to be one measurement.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Negative energy density made operational.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  Van Casteel's Cumbrian measurement is the payoff of Chapter Fifteen's Morris\endash 
Thorne traversability condition: a wormhole throat held open by exotic (negative-energy) matter will imprint a detectable sign correction on the local vacuum, measurable by the s
ame Casimir-plate apparatus used throughout the book (Chapters 2, 8, 12, 15). The chapter treats this as van Casteel would: not proof of an interpretation, but a force, in attometres, that either exists or does not.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 The narrow-throat information bound.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  
A wormhole throat with Planck- or nuclear-scale cross-section, however long-lived, has a bounded channel capacity \emdash 
 this is a direct consequence of treating the throat as any other narrow channel in information theory, where capacity scales with cross-sec
tional geometry. The chapter's claim that the fourteen's "missing residue" (Chapter One) matches this bound to instrument error is fiction built on a real constraint: a real physical channel this narrow could carry only a slow trickle of correlations, nev
er matter, and never at a rate usable for communication or travel \emdash  which is exactly why Schwarzschild's and Esaki's cautions from Chapters 14\endash 15 both survive intact rather than being overturned by a "solved" wormhole.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Self-reference and undecidability, resolved by re-description rather than proof.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  The nine undecidable cores from Chapter Six's G\'f6
del-sentence motion are not resolved by finding a clever proof; they are resolved by recognising that the department was asking a system to certify a definite yes/no fact about objects that are not, physically, in a single definite location \emdash 
 a category error the formal undecidability was quietly encoding all along. This mirrors a real methodological lesson from G\'f6del and Turing's work: undecidability results often indicate that the question was ill-posed for 
the system being asked, not that the system is defective.
\par }{\rtlch\fcs1 \ab\af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \cs37\b\insrsid14645859 Discharge and liability, in the register's own terms.}{\rtlch\fcs1 \af0 \ltrch\fcs0 \insrsid14645859  Chapter Twelve 
established that Weinstein's stored entropy could not be discharged without releasing it into whatever room he stood in. Chapter Sixteen does not repeal that law \emdash 
 it identifies a discharge pathway (the throat) that was, unbeknownst to the department, ope
rating the entire time, converting an apparently unpayable Landauer debt into an ordinary, measurable, insurable outflow. Nothing in the resolution violates the thermodynamics established four chapters earlier; it simply locates the missing term in the ba
lance sheet.
